diff --git a/docs/head-and-enrollment-limits.md b/docs/head-and-enrollment-limits.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..a14b640
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/head-and-enrollment-limits.md
@@ -0,0 +1,96 @@
+# Head reports and total enrollment
+
+3 October 2026. The collector requires two additional explicit settings:
+
+| Setting | Meaning |
+| --- | --- |
+| `COLLECTOR_MAX_HEAD_BYTES` | Maximum UTF-8 JSON size of a projected complete head, before collector timing fields |
+| `COLLECTOR_MAX_NODES` | Maximum number of identities in the startup credential file, including disconnected identities |
+
+Both are positive integers, with no defaults. A credential roster exceeding the
+enrollment limit stops startup before listener construction. Two credentials for
+one identity during rotation consume one slot. Credentials are loaded once;
+changing the roster requires the existing collector restart procedure, which
+also clears its in-memory node/history inventory. All retained inactive nodes
+therefore remain within the same roster bound. The TCP connection cap is separate.
+Set persistence `SNAPSHOT_MAX_NODES` at least as high as the collector roster cap.
+Separate process environments are not automatically cross-validated.
+
+Complete `block` and combined `update` reports are structurally validated, then
+projected to the supported native fields. Every transaction hash and supported
+uncle-header field is retained. Unknown head, transaction and uncle extensions
+are discarded; their incoming bytes still count toward the whole-message cap.
+The projection reuses the existing scalar chart-field mapping, adding complete
+transaction hashes and uncle headers instead of chart counts. It has fixed
+object depth; it does not serialize arbitrary nested extensions.
+
+If that projected head exceeds the configured limit, the entire report is
+rejected before head, history or receipt mutation. Transaction lists are never
+truncated. The session can submit a later acceptable report, and the ordinary
+freshness rules expire the previous head in the meantime. History backfill keeps
+its existing structural validation, fifty-block limit, input byte cap and compact
+chart representation; it does not retain complete historical transaction arrays.
+
+Registration also checks that the Primus-derived address is an IPv4/IPv6 literal
+of at most 45 characters, without an interface scope suffix. The IP added after
+the earlier 2048-byte metadata projection therefore contributes at most 53
+additional JSON bytes. This bounds the field, not its authenticity. Proxy header
+normalization remains required; dashboard geolocation is not peer verification.
+
+## Tested candidate profile
+
+The measured candidate uses 8 enrolled identities, 65536-byte heads, 4 MiB input,
+1 MiB pending output and 1 MiB snapshot messages. These are explicit test values,
+not automatically selected defaults or final production approval. Larger valid
+chain blocks can need a larger dashboard budget; remeasure coordinated limits
+before increasing enrollment or supported report size. Dashboard rejection does
+not change block acceptance, signing, mining or peer admission in efsn.
+
+A sizing run registers eight 64-character identities sequentially under a
+three-TCP-connection cap. Metadata includes U+2028 to exercise Primus JSON
+escaping. Each has exactly 2048 bytes of metadata and 65536 bytes
+of projected head data, including all 714 hashes. All eight disconnect and remain
+in inventory. One reconnects using its rotating credential and replaces its head
+without adding a row. Both API routes preserve complete native fields. Oversized
+single/combined reports leave heads, receipts and chart cache unchanged. These
+are synthetic telemetry objects; padded uncle extra data exercises byte admission
+and is not evidence of consensus-valid uncle headers or chain throughput.
+
+| Quantity | Boundary run |
+| --- | ---: |
+| Fifty-head history message | 3,276,956 bytes |
+| Largest inventory message | 561,635 bytes |
+| Largest stored snapshot JSON | 554,917 bytes |
+| Chart message | 8,798 bytes |
+| HTTP `/nodes` | 589,075 bytes |
+| HTTP `/blocks` | 549,897 bytes |
+
+Both the all-inactive and reconnected snapshots save successfully with zero
+writer errors or output rejections. The prior eight-concurrent-reporter workload
+also passes with these new guards and four advancing snapshots. These runs do
+not simulate public viewer fan-out, arbitrary queued traffic or a prolonged soak.
+The output guard can still disconnect a slow consumer whose queued writes exceed
+its cap even when each individual inventory fits.
+
+For budget review, allow `N * (H + 8192) + 32768` bytes for one inventory plus
+charts under the current schema and locked dependencies: 622592 bytes for this
+profile. The 8 KiB per-node allowance covers up to 4149 wire bytes for metadata/IP
+(including Primus U+2028/U+2029 escaping), 1024 for GeoIP, 1001 for forty
+propagation numbers, 1024 for scalar stats/uptime/receipts, 256 for added timing,
+and 256 for identity/socket ID and outer JSON structure: 7710 bytes in total. The 32 KiB chart allowance covers forty-entry
+scalar arrays (including 78-digit difficulties), histogram bins and two miners.
+This is a conservative schema-based sizing allowance, not a runtime queue/memory
+guarantee. Include frame overhead and queued events when setting transport limits;
+repeat the review if schemas, plugins or dependency versions change.
+
+Validation: six new regressions fail on the previous source; 173 contracts and
+13 existing socket tests pass. Boundary enrollment, concurrent eight-reporter
+and actual efsn/database/API/browser checks pass. The original failed boundary
+attempt expected unknown (`null`) freshness on disconnected known reports;
+the existing API correctly returns zero. Only the test expectation was corrected.
+All five disposable databases are stopped and password files removed.
+
+Review evidence: node repository
+`docs/evidence/restart-dashboard-enrollment-2026-10-03/`.
+Next deployment work: selected proxy/TLS routing and forwarding headers,
+collector loss during mining, runtime/dependency review and supervision.
